austed-without-body... actually for coverage.py branch coverage, a `for` loop has arcs: (line4 \u2192 line5) for body execution and (line4 \u2192 line7) for loop exhaustion. is_prime(2): 4\u21927. is_prime(4): 4\u21925, 5\u21926. is_prime(5): 4\u21925, 5\u21924 (loop back), 4\u21927. So all arcs covered with a=2's full run: is_prime called with 2 (4\u21927 direct), 3 (4\u21925, 5 False, 4\u21927), 4 (5\u21926), etc.\n\n2. Line 5: `if n%j == 0` \u2014 True (n=4) and False (n=3,5,...). Covered.\n\n3. Line 13: `if not is_prime(i)` \u2014 True (i=4 \u2192 continue) and False (i=2 \u2192 proceed). Covered by full loop.\n\n4. Line 15: same. Covered.\n\n5. Line 17: same. Covered.\n\n6. Line 18: `if i*j*k == a` \u2014 False (a=2, never equal) and True (a=8). Covered by two tests.\n\n7. Line 12/14/16 for loops: normal exit (a=2 full run) and... early exit via return (a=8). For branch coverage of for loops, arcs: loop\u2192body, loop\u2192exit. Both covered.\n\nSo two tests suffice:\n- `assert is_multiply_prime(8) is True` \u2014 covers line 18 True branch.\n- `assert is_multiply_prime(2) is False` \u2014 covers everything else including line 19.\n\nHmm wait, but with a=8, does it cover line 18 True? i=2, j=2, k=2: 8==8 \u2192 return True. Yes.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 does the a=8 test cover line 5? is_prime(2) only: range(2,2) empty \u2192 line 4\u21927. So line 5 not covered by a=8 test, but covered by a=2 test. Combined, full coverage. \n\nBut actually, maybe I should add a=30 too for good measure and to make tests more meaningful (distinct primes). It also covers line 18 False branch before True. Not strictly necessary but nice. Each additional full-ish loop test adds runtime. a=30: i=2, j=2, k=2..5 \u2192 quick. Cheap. Let me include it.\n\nAlso maybe test a=1 (edge) \u2014 full loop, returns False. That's another slow full loop. Maybe skip or include one more False case. Let me think about what's reasonable: the task says \"execute all missing lines and branches\". Two or